Comments and questions on Tim May speculation concerning intelligence
activities in Monterey.

In '78-79 probably 70% of the students at the Defense Language
Institute were radio intercept operators, 15% were POW interogators,
and the remaining 15% were officers about to be assigned overseas
and spooks.

The vast majority studied Russian, German, Chinese, and Korean (in
approximate descending order).

Is the "Monterey Institute for International Studies" the same as
the "Naval Institute for Post-graduate Studies" ? Gordon Eubanks,
of Symantec, developed his C-basic compiler while there in the '70s.
I seem to remember that Gary Kildall's Digital Research, and in
particular the CP/M OS, grew out of his doctoral research there.

I wasn't aware of the Ft. Ord -> Navy Oceanic Computing Center
connection; back then it was just the 7th Infantry. To my knowledge,
the SigInt stuff was all done elsewhere then. Most of the human int.
and satellite training was done at Ft. Huachuca (sp?) in AZ, and the
traffic analysis was done at an air force base in TX (the name of
which escapes me at the moment).

It isn't very surprising that AT&T would put a translation service
in Monterey. The language resources in the area are _very_ extensive
(because of DLI) and many of the instructors are only part time, so
moonlighting for AT&T is probably quite attractive.

          Jeff

