From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: UNIX: Be thankful for what you've got

freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman) writes:

>> >I _hate_ UNIX!
>> >
>> >--Tim
>> 
>> "I wept because I used UNIX, until I met a man who used
>> VMS."

>    Someone send these poor computationally challenged souls copies of OS/2.

A guru said unto me many years ago, "UNIX is dead but the corpse
hasn't begun to stink yet." But I was callow and young and unwise in
the ways of the Software Industry, and I ignored his words of wisdom.

And lo it came to pass that I went to work for the house of SCO, on the
UNIX technical documentation team.  And I did hear much wailing and
gnashing of teeth as I laboured mightily in the very pits of gehenna.
Lo, verily I say unto you: UNIX has been dead for years and the corpse
rotteth odiferously.  But lo, necrophiliacs gather from far and wide to
wonder at the mortal remnants: and they attempteth to preserve it in the
name of Open Systems Standards.

And it will come to pass that the Open Systems Standards will take on
a life of their own and, shambling, stumble forth from the tomb into
the light of day to terrorize whosoever goeth in the market place. And
there will be much wailing and gnashing of teeth from the Users, and
they will cry out to the Administrators: "why hast thou forsaken us?
Why dost thou inflict upon us this gangrenous imitation of a living,
breathing system? Why must we use this rotting carcass that was
obsolete a decade ago?"

And the Administrators shall gather together in a high place like unto
USENIX, and they will say until the Users: "tough shit, buddy, you 
asked for compatability and open standards in the first place!"

And verily and forsooth there will be a Great Stench upon the waters.

The point I'm trying to make is: UNIX accretes functionality over time.
In fact, what we _call_ UNIX today is a vast mass of gunge pasted on top
of what was a small, elegant research system in the 70's.  Things are
_added_ to UNIX but they are never taken away; so it's riddled with
arcane and archaic features.  It should have been killed mercifully a
few years ago and replaced with something like Plan 9, but now it's
preparing to steamroller everything in its path, on the back of the Open
Standards movement.  Even Bill Gates has been quoted as saying in public
that "Windows NT is just another kind of UNIX".

Heh. Compatability just makes it easier for Them to sieze your system
and go stomping through it with hob-nailed file scanners. _Real_
cypherpunks run PGP on an embedded 80186 in their washing machine, and
program it in septadecimal using front-panel toggle switches.




-- Charlie (merrily sawing away at the branch he's sitting on)

